    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen tablets and capsules are the most convenient format and the least likely
        to reach a dose that has been studied.</strong> A tablet holds roughly 500mg to 1g of
        collagen, while the trials that reduced joint pain used 5g to 10g a day, so a tablet
        route means swallowing 10 to 20 of them daily. What that leaves you with:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tablets and capsules:</strong> easy to carry, but 2 tablets a day gives you about 1g, roughly a tenth of the dose used in the 24-week athlete trial.</li>
        <li><strong>Sachets and powders:</strong> the dose is right (usually 5g to 10g per sachet) and the cost per gram is the lowest of any format. Mixing every single morning is where people fall off.</li>
        <li><strong>Gummies:</strong> typically 1g to 2.5g of collagen across two or three gummies, usually with added sugar. The weakest option on dose per rupee.</li>
        <li><strong>Liquid shots:</strong> a full 10g pre-dissolved in 30ml, nothing to mix, but honestly the highest cost per serving of the five. If your budget is tight, a plain powder at a studied dose beats our shot on value.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

