      <h2 id="dose-math">The arithmetic behind collagen tablets</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen tablets fail on volume, not on quality. Collagen is the structural protein that
        makes up roughly a third of the protein in your body, and it is dosed in grams rather than
        milligrams, which is the opposite of how most supplements behave. A vitamin D tablet works
        at 600 IU because the body needs micrograms of it. Collagen needs thousands of times more
        material, and a tablet is a physically small object.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is the constraint nobody in the category likes to spell out. A tablet a human can
        comfortably swallow tops out at about 1,000mg to 1,500mg of total weight, and part of that
        weight is binder, anti-caking agent and coating. So the actual collagen inside a typical
        tablet or capsule is <strong>500mg to 1,000mg</strong>. Most bottles on Indian marketplaces
        recommend two a day. That is 1g to 2g of collagen.
      </p>

      <p>
        The trials that produced the results these products are marketed on used far more. Clark
        and colleagues gave 147 athletes 10g of collagen hydrolysate daily for 24 weeks.
        Zdzieblik and colleagues used 5g a day for 12 weeks in young adults with knee discomfort.
        Skin trials cluster in the same range, usually 2.5g to 10g. To reach the lower end of that
        band from a 500mg tablet you would take ten tablets a day. At the upper end, twenty.
      </p>

      <p>
        I have never met a patient who did that for three months. In my OPD I have met plenty who
        took two tablets a day for three months, felt nothing, and concluded that collagen does not
        work. They did not test collagen. They tested a tenth of a dose.
      </p>

