      <p>
        Read that table honestly and powder wins on value. It is the format I recommend to patients
        who are paying out of pocket for a family of four and cannot justify a per-serving product.
        A plain unflavoured marine or bovine peptide powder at 10g a day, taken in coffee or nimbu
        pani, delivers a studied dose for the least money. If you want the peptide chemistry behind
        why any of these forms work at all, that is covered separately in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides: what they are and how they work</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reason powder does not win outright is behavioural rather than biochemical. Sachets sit
        in the kitchen. Kitchens are busy at 7:30 in the morning. The powder that is not mixed is
        a 0g dose, and 0g has never beaten placebo in any trial.
      </p>

      <h2 id="absorption">Does the form change how well collagen is absorbed?</h2>

      <p>
        No, and this is the single most oversold claim in the category. Once a collagen peptide is
        dissolved in the gut, the body does not know or care whether it arrived as a tablet, a
        powder or a liquid. Hydrolysed collagen is already broken into short peptide chains, and
        those chains are absorbed through the small intestine the same way regardless of what they
        travelled in.
      </p>

      <p>
        The "liquid is 90% absorbed, tablets only 20%" line you see in ads is borrowed from
        discussions of tablet disintegration for poorly soluble drugs. Collagen peptides are highly
        water soluble. A tablet has to disintegrate first, which takes minutes, and a liquid skips
        that step, but skipping a few minutes is not the same as absorbing more. There is no
        head-to-head human trial showing that the same dose of the same collagen peptide produces
        higher blood peptide levels from a liquid than from a capsule. If a brand claims otherwise,
        ask them for the citation.
      </p>

      <p>
        What genuinely changes absorption is peptide size, not packaging. Low molecular weight
        hydrolysates cross the gut wall more readily than intact collagen or gelatin, and studies by
        Iwai and colleagues in 2005 detected hydroxyproline-containing peptides in human blood after
        oral collagen, which is how we know the peptides survive digestion at all. That work says
        nothing about tablets versus shots. It says hydrolysis matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the honest framing is this: <strong>form does not change absorption, form changes the
        dose you take and how many days you take it.</strong> Those two things decide outcomes.
        Everything else in this argument is marketing.
      </p>

