      <h2 id="gummies">Are collagen gummies worth buying?</h2>

      <p>
        For most people, no. A collagen gummy carries somewhere between 300mg and 1,000mg of
        collagen and needs 2g to 5g of sugar or sugar alcohol to hold its shape and taste like
        something you want to chew. To reach 10g of collagen from gummies you would eat ten to
        thirty of them and take on 20g to 100g of sugar in the process.
      </p>

      <p>
        In India this matters more than it does in most markets. The ICMR-INDIAB survey published
        in <em>The Lancet Diabetes and Endocrinology</em> in 2023 estimated 101 million people
        living with diabetes and another 136 million with prediabetes. A daily sugar-carrying
        supplement is a poor default in a population with that background risk. If you have insulin
        resistance, PCOS or a family history of type 2 diabetes, gummies are the wrong format
        regardless of how good the collagen inside them is.
      </p>

      <p>
        Gummies do have one legitimate use. If a person genuinely cannot swallow tablets or will not
        drink anything with a protein taste, a small daily dose taken reliably still beats a large
        dose that never gets taken. I would rather someone take 1g a day of something than nothing
        at all. Just be clear with yourself that you are buying compliance, not the dose used in the
        trials.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cost">What does each format cost per month in India?</h2>

      <p>
        Cost per gram of collagen is the only comparison that means anything, and by that measure
        plain powder is cheapest and gummies are most expensive. Retail listings shift constantly,
        so treat the ranges below as the shape of the market rather than a price list.
      </p>

      <p>
        Work it through with a 10g daily target. Bulk unflavoured peptide powder in India generally
        lands somewhere around INR 4 to 8 per gram of collagen, which puts a studied dose in the
        region of INR 1,200 to 2,400 a month. Tablets look cheap on the shelf, often INR 600 to
        1,200 for a bottle, but that bottle holds 60 tablets at 500mg, which is 30g of collagen in
        total. Three days of a studied dose. Priced per gram, tablets are frequently the second
        most expensive way to buy collagen in the country, which is the opposite of how they are
        perceived.
      </p>

      <p>
        A liquid shot costs more per serving than any of these except gummies, and I am not going to
        pretend otherwise. You are paying for water, glass, cold chain-free stability, flavour work
        and the co-factors dissolved alongside the collagen. What you are buying with that premium
        is the absence of a decision at 7:30 in the morning.
      </p>

