      <h2 id="why-shot">Why we chose a shot, and what it costs you</h2>

      <p>
        We chose a 30ml liquid because it removes every step between deciding to take collagen and
        having taken it. That is the only advantage the format has, and it is a real one, but it is
        not free.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a> holds <strong>10,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed
        marine collagen</strong> in a single 30ml shot, which is 10g of protein at zero sugar and
        40 kcal. That is the Clark trial dose in one serving rather than in twenty tablets. Around
        it sit <strong>300mg glucosamine HCl and 120mg hyaluronic acid</strong> for joint structure
        and lubrication, <strong>250mg calcium, 600 IU vitamin D3 and 50mg magnesium</strong> for
        the bone underneath, <strong>500mg Cissus quadrangularis and 1,000mg L-carnitine</strong>
        for muscle and endurance, and an antioxidant group of 100mg rose hip extract, 60mg CoQ10,
        40mg vitamin C from amla and 2mg lycopene. Thirteen actives, all in solution, none of which
        would fit inside a tablet alongside 10g of collagen.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now the inconvenient part. Two of those doses sit below the levels used in the monotherapy
        trials for those specific ingredients. Glucosamine trials used 1,500mg daily and Skelecoll
        has 300mg, so it is a co-factor in a stack rather than a glucosamine therapy, as I set out
        in more detail in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>.
        Vitamin D at 600 IU is a maintenance amount, not a correction dose. If your 25-hydroxy
        vitamin D is genuinely low, and in urban India it very often is, you need a separate
        prescribed regimen and a blood test, not a shot.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is also a format limitation worth naming. You cannot titrate a shot. Somebody who
        wants to start at 5g and build up can do that with powder trivially and cannot do it with a
        sealed 30ml bottle without wasting half of it. And per month, a daily shot will cost you
        more than a tub of plain peptides. If cost per gram is your deciding variable, buy the tub.
        We would rather say that here than have you find it out later.
      </p>

