      <h2 id="choose">How to pick the format you will actually finish</h2>

      <p>
        Pick on two questions only: does one serving reach 5g or more, and will you take it every
        day for twelve weeks without negotiating with yourself. Everything else is secondary.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>You are price-sensitive and disciplined.</strong> Buy plain hydrolysed peptide powder at 10g a day. Best value in the category by a wide margin. Check the label states grams of collagen per scoop, not per 100g.</li>
        <li><strong>You have skipped supplements before.</strong> A single-serving format, shot or single-dose sachet, is worth the premium purely because it survives contact with a real morning.</li>
        <li><strong>You travel constantly.</strong> Sealed single servings beat a tub and scoop. Tablets travel best of all, but see the dose problem above.</li>
        <li><strong>You are vegetarian.</strong> No collagen supplement is vegetarian. All of it comes from fish, bovine or poultry sources, and "vegan collagen" products contain amino acids and vitamin C that support your own collagen synthesis rather than collagen itself. That is a different product with different evidence, covered in <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">how to boost collagen naturally</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>You have diabetes or insulin resistance.</strong> Rule out gummies. Check flavoured powders for maltodextrin, which is common and pushes the glycaemic load up quietly.</li>
        <li><strong>You have a fish or shellfish allergy.</strong> Marine collagen is out entirely. Read the source on the label, not the front of the pack. The differences between sources are set out in <a href="/pages/marine-collagen">marine collagen: benefits, dose and how to choose</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Two label checks before you pay for anything. First, find the milligrams of collagen per
        serving and the number of servings, then divide. If the label gives a proprietary blend
        weight without breaking out the collagen, assume the collagen number is unflattering.
        Second, check the FSSAI licence number on the pack. It is a legal requirement for
        nutraceuticals sold in India and its absence on an imported listing tells you the product
        arrived through a channel with no local accountability.
      </p>

      <p>
        Judge whichever format you choose at twelve weeks, not at three. Collagen works slowly in
        every trial that has ever shown it working at all, and the format debate is only worth
        having because it decides whether you make it to week twelve.
      </p>

