      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How many collagen tablets should I take a day?</summary>
          <p>
            To match the 5g to 10g doses used in published trials you would need roughly 10 to 20
            tablets a day, since a typical tablet holds 500mg to 1,000mg of collagen. Most bottles
            recommend two, which delivers 1g to 2g. If you want a studied dose, a powder or a
            liquid format is a more practical route than tablets.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are collagen tablets or liquid collagen better?</summary>
          <p>
            Liquid is better for reaching a studied dose, not for absorption. A 30ml shot can carry
            10,000mg of pre-dissolved collagen in one serving while a tablet carries under 1,000mg,
            but once dissolved, the peptides are absorbed the same way from either. The real
            difference is dose per serving and how likely you are to keep taking it.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do collagen capsules actually work?</summary>
          <p>
            They work only if you take enough of them, and almost nobody does. Collagen capsules
            hold about 500mg each, so the two-a-day serving on most labels gives you around a tenth
            of the 10g used in the 24-week Clark athlete trial. The ingredient is not the problem,
            the quantity is.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are collagen gummies worth it?</summary>
          <p>
            For most people, no. Gummies typically hold 300mg to 1,000mg of collagen each and carry
            2g to 5g of sugar per serving, so reaching 10g of collagen would mean a large sugar
            load. They make sense only for someone who will not take any other format.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is a collagen shot worth the extra cost over powder?</summary>
          <p>
            Only if convenience is what stops you being consistent. Plain peptide powder delivers
            the same 10g dose at a lower cost per gram, and if your budget is tight it is the better
            buy. A shot is worth the premium when the daily mixing step is the reason your last tub
            went unfinished.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do collagen sachets work as well as a shot?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, on dose they are equivalent. A sachet holding 5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen
            delivers the same peptides as a shot of the same size once it is dissolved. The
            trade-off is preparation, since a sachet needs water, stirring and a clean glass every
            morning.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

